The '''TL-50 Heavy Repeater''', also known as the '''TL-50 Heavy Blaster''', was a model of [[Heavy repeating blaster|heavy repeating]] [[blaster rifle]] that was manufactured for the [[Imperial Special Forces|special forces]] of the [[Galactic Empire]]. In addition to sending storms of [[blaster]] bolts from its multiple [[barrel]]s, the [[Heavy blaster rifle|rifle]] could gather its energy into a powerful [[Concussion rifle|concussion blast]]. It could also be modified with an extended barrel for reduced spread and with a [[power cell]] for increased cooling power. During the [[Galactic Civil War]] against the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]], [[Commander]] [[Iden Versio]] of the Empire's [[Special forces|elite]] [[Inferno Squad]] carried a TL-50 Heavy Repeater.

==Description==
{{Listen3|TL-50-Blast.flac|TL-50 Heavy Repeater releasing a concussion blast}}
[[File:TL-50_modified.png|thumb|right|310px|A double-barreled TL-50 modified with an extended main barrel and a power cell for greater cooling power]]
The TL-50 Heavy Repeater,<ref name="BF" /> otherwise known as the TL-50 Heavy Blaster<ref name="AMG-Databank">{{AMGweb|url=transmission/databank-download-inferno-squad|text=Databank Download: Inferno Squad|date=2024-04-30}}</ref> or simply the TL-50 Repeater,<ref name="Legion">{{Legion|set=Iden Versio and ID10 Commander Expansion|cardname=Iden's TL-50 Repeater}}</ref> was a model of [[Heavy repeating blaster|heavy repeating]] [[blaster rifle]] that was capable of discharging storms of [[Color|red]]-colored [[blaster]] bolts.<ref name="BF" /> Thanks to the [[Heavy blaster rifle|rifle's]] reinforced casing with built-in [[power cell]],<ref name="BF II" /> it could additionally gather energy to release a lethal [[Concussion rifle|concussion blast]] as secondary fire.<ref name="BF" />

The standard TL-50 Heavy Repeater was black in color and consisted of triple [[barrel]]s, arrayed in a triangular pattern. The [[weapon]]'s frame contained cooling vents housed around the triple barrels, with a sight positioned at the top. Two horizontal tubes underneath the weapon connected the front of the [[rifle]] to the dark-green [[stock]]. The blaster rifle additionally possessed a trigger guard.<ref name="BF" />

The [[repeating blaster]] also featured a variant equipped with dual barrels but without the concussion-blast ability. Unlike the standard TL-50, the modified version lacked the horizontal tubes on the underside of the weapon. The TL-50 could additionally be modified with an extended main barrel featuring a larger sight that allowed for greater precision during sustained fire, along with a power cell attached to one side of the weapon, which produced two red lights, for superior cooling power.<ref name="BF II" />

==History==
[[File:Iden_TL-50.png|thumb|left|285px|The TL-50 was favored by Iden Versio of Inferno Squad.]]
The TL-50 Heavy Repeater was manufactured for [[Imperial Special Forces]] during the [[Imperial Era|reign]] of the [[Galactic Empire]].<ref name="BF II" /> Sometime between the [[Standard year|years]] [[19 BBY]] and [[10 BBY]],<ref name="SG note">According to ''[[Star Wars: Smuggler's Guide]]'', [[Masse Goskey]] and [[Ephant Mon]]'s [[weapon]]s purchase took place some [[time]] after the [[Proclamation of the New Order|formation]] of the [[Galactic Empire]]. Mon also mentioned Goskey's [[death]] in the [[in-universe]] ''[[Smuggler's Guide]]'', which later came into possession of [[Dryden Vos]], meaning Goskey died before Vos did. The weapons purchase must have therefore occurred between the Empire's formation and [[Showdown on Savareen|Vos's death]], which ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates to [[19 BBY]] and [[10 BBY]], respectively.</ref> [[Masse Goskey]], the proprietor of [[Masse Goskey's Arms Emporium and Explosives Mart]]<ref name="SG" /> in [[Mos Eisley]] [[spaceport]] on the planet [[Tatooine]],<ref name="CL">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref> and his associate [[Ephant Mon]] [[Arms dealer|purchased]] a TL-50 for 3,200 [[Galactic Credit Standard|credits]]. Mon recorded the transaction in a [[book]] that would eventually be known as the ''[[Smuggler's Guide]]''.<ref name="SG" />

In [[3 ABY]],<ref name="Outlaws date">{{OutlawsDate}}</ref> an individual wielded a TL-50 Heavy Repeater on a [[speeder bike]] while chasing the outlaw [[Kay Vess]] and her<ref name="Outlaws trailer">{{SWYouTube|XF0kMT39GNY|Star Wars Outlaws: World Premiere Trailer|sw_url=video/world-premiere-trailer-star-wars-outlaws|date=2023-06-11}}</ref> [[merqaal]]<ref name="Merqaal">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-outlaws-interview|text=SDCC 2023: "Step Into the World of ''Star Wars''": Julian Gerighty Talks ''Star Wars Outlaws''|author=[[Dan Brooks|Brooks, Dan]]|date=2023-07-23|quote=Nix is a merqaal.}}</ref> companion, [[Nix]], through a barren landscape.<ref name="Outlaws trailer" /> During the same year an individual in [[Kijimi City]] on the [[Outer Rim Territories]] [[planet]] [[Kijimi]] placed a rush order for a number of weapons including both a stock and a barrel replacement for a TL-50; promising an extra ten percent in payment if the order could be filled by the end of the week.<ref name="Outlaws" />

Another TL-50 Heavy Repeater was used by [[Commander]] [[Iden Versio]], who served as the [[Commanding officer|leader]] of the Empire's [[Special forces|special operations]] team [[Inferno Squad]] during the [[Galactic Civil War]] against the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]], until her [[Skirmish on Vardos|defection]] during [[Operation: Cinder]]<ref name="BF II" /> in 4 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|ABY]].<ref name="SWT">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> Her Heavy Repeater was the standard-issue TL-50 with triple barrels, albeit modified with a power cell.<ref name="BF II" /> A [[stormtrooper]] [[squad leader]] also used a TL-50 Heavy Repeater on one occasion.<ref name="SWD note">{{Destiny|set=Spark of Hope|cardname=TL-50 Heavy Repeater|link=https://swdestinydb.com/card/11037}} depicts a TL-50 Heavy Repeater wielded by an [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[stormtrooper]] [[Clothing|wearing]] an [[Color|orange]] [[pauldron]], which denotes the [[Military rank|rank]] of [[squad leader]], according to {{InsiderCite|158|Rebel Bluff}}.</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
===''Star Wars Battlefront''===
{{Quote|''Star Wars''™ Battlefront adds the TL-50 Heavy Repeater and K-16 Bryar Pistol to the blaster arsenal in Death Star. Keen fans might recognize these blasters as a nod to the classic ''Star Wars'' video game series, Jedi Knight.|Official [[Electronic Arts]] website|<ref name="BF Death Star">{{EA|subdomain=starwars|url=starwars/battlefront/news/death-star-new-weapons-and-star-cards|text=Death Star New Weapons + Star Cards|date=2016-09-21|nolive=1}}</ref>}}
[[File:TL-50_concept.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Concept art of the TL-50 Heavy Repeater by Erik Rading]]
The TL-50 Heavy Repeater first appeared in the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]] expansion pack for the [[Star Wars video games|video game]] ''[[Star Wars Battlefront]]'', released in [[2016]].<ref name="BF Death Star" /> The rifle is unlockable via an [[W:c:battlefront:Hutt Contract|in-game challenge]] named "Imperial Instrument," which required getting twenty [[Death|kills]] playing as an Empire [[soldier]], accumulating seventy-five heavy-blaster kills, and winning a round of the "[[W:c:battlefront:Battle Station|Battle Station]]" game mode. However, like all other blasters in the game, the TL-50 can also be wielded by [[rebel trooper]]s and [[New Republic soldier]]s, as well as Imperial stormtroopers.<ref name="BF" />

While the [[2018]] reference book ''[[Star Wars: Smuggler's Guide]]'', written by [[Daniel Wallace]], does not capitalize the weapon's name and renders it as "TL-50 heavy repeater,"<ref name="SG" /> the [[2020]] ''[[Iden Versio and ID10 Commander Expansion]]'' for the ''[[Star Wars: Legion]]'' miniatures game identifies the blaster simply as "TL-50 Repeater."<ref name="Legion" /> On [[April 30]], [[2024]], a "Databank Download" preview for<ref name="AMG-Databank" /> ''[[Today the Rebellion Dies Squad Pack]]'', a [[June 7]], 2024 expansion for [[Atomic Mass Games]]' [[Star Wars: Shatterpoint|''Star Wars'': Shatterpoint]] miniatures skirmish game,<ref name="Asmodee">{{Asmodee|url=products/star-wars-shatterpoint-today-the-rebellion-dies-squad-pack|text=Star Wars: Shatterpoint - Today the Rebellion Dies Squad Pack}}</ref> introduced the name "TL-50 Heavy Blaster" for the weapon.<ref name="AMG-Databank" />

The TL-50 was designed by 3D artist [[Erik Rading]]<ref name="Rading">{{ArtStation|subdomain=erikrading|url=projects/J32Pa|name=[[Erik Rading]]|text=Star Wars Battlefront - Heavy Repeater|quote=One of the weapons I made while I was an intern at [[DICE|dice]] [''sic'']. I Was [''sic''] also responsible for the concept and marketing renders.|archivedate=20181011025544|nolive=1}}</ref> based on the [[Imperial Heavy Repeater]] from<ref name="BF Death Star" /> the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity's [[2002]] video game ''[[Star Wars: Jedi Knight II: Jedi Outcast]]''.<ref name="JK2">''[[Star Wars: Jedi Knight II: Jedi Outcast]]''</ref> On the other hand, ''Star Wars: Smuggler's Guide'' presents the TL-50 heavy repeater and the [[Imperial heavy repeater]] as separate items in an [[in-universe]] list of weaponry, effectively rendering them as two distinct weapons in the current [[canon]].<ref name="SG" />

===''Star Wars Battlefront II'' and ''The Mandalorian''===
{{Quote|I did a blue Mandalorian and a red one, which I quite liked. ''[&hellip;] [The blue one]'' felt the most action figure-y, I think.|Brian Matyas|<ref name="Mando Art">[[The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian (Season One)|''The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One]]</ref>}}
[[File:TL-50_stats.png|thumb|left|275px|The TL-50 Heavy Repeater's statistics in ''Star Wars Battlefront'' (top) and ''Star Wars Battlefront II'' (bottom)]]
The TL-50 also appeared in ''Star Wars Battlefront''{{'s}} [[2017]] sequel, ''[[Star Wars Battlefront II]]'', as a blaster option exclusively for the [[W:c:battlefront:Heavy (class)|heavy trooper class]], such as [[Heavy Battle Droid|heavy battle droids]], [[heavy weapons clone trooper]]s, [[Heavy Weapons Stormtrooper|heavy stormtroopers]], [[Heavy Soldier|heavy rebel soldiers]], and [[First Order heavy trooper|megablaster heavy assault troopers]]. The weapon is available for heavy [[trooper]]s from all factions, including the [[Galactic Republic]], the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems|Separatist Alliance]], the Galactic Empire, the Rebel Alliance, the [[First Order]], and the [[Resistance]]. The blaster can be unlocked by defeating 500 enemies while playing as the heavy class.<ref name="BF II" />

The blaster is also the sole primary weapon of the "hero" character Iden Versio in multiplayer mode. However, in the single-player campaign, in which she is the protagonist, Versio's weapon is chosen by the player. The campaign's cutscenes additionally depict her wielding an [[E-11 medium blaster rifle]] as an Imperial.<ref name="BF II" />

[[File:ClanMandoVersion3A.jpg|thumb|right|180px|"Clan Mando Version 3A" by Brian Matyas]]
In ''Battlefront II'', the TL-50's range is significantly reduced in comparison to the previous ''Battlefront''.<ref name="BF" /><ref name="BF II" /> Although the blaster's secondary fire is a default and permanent feature in ''Battlefront''<ref name="BF" /> and the campaign of ''Battlefront II'', it appears as an unlockable modification for the sequel game's multiplayer modes. Along with the extended barrel and the power cell to increase cooling power, the player can only equip two of these three modifications at the same time in multiplayer.<ref name="BF II" />

For the flashback sequences featuring<ref name="Mando Art" /> the [[Attack on Aq Vetina|attack]] on [[Din Djarin]]'s [[Homeworld|home planet]] of [[Aq Vetina]]<ref name="Djarin-Encyclopedia">{{EncyclopediaCite|Din Djarin and Other Characters of the Underworld}}</ref> in ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'' live-action series' [[The Mandalorian Season One|first season]], various concept designs for [[Death Watch Warrior|Death Watch soldiers]] were created. One of those designs by concept artist [[Brian Matyas]], titled "Clan Mando Version 3A," depicted a [[Mandalorian warrior]] wearing blue-colored [[Mandalorian armor|armor]] and wielding a TL-50 Heavy Repeater, which was the design that resembled the most to an action figure according to Matyas.<ref name="Mando Art" />

